1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,720
How can ecom take a card payment without ever seeing the card?

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:10,560
In Lesson 4, card numbers went into one locked room, and ecom kept only tickets: card tokens.

3
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:15,696
In Lesson 1, the buyer typed the card on the bank's payment page, straight into the card zone.

4
00:00:15,746 --> 00:00:22,056
This lesson shows how that page and the locked room work together, so ecom never sees the card.

5
00:00:22,106 --> 00:00:23,928
Think of a real shop.

6
00:00:23,978 --> 00:00:27,720
When you pay, the cashier turns the card reader towards you.

7
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:30,672
That reader belongs to the bank, not the shop.

8
00:00:30,722 --> 00:00:35,232
You type your PIN into the bank's machine, and the shop only gets a receipt.

9
00:00:35,282 --> 00:00:37,344
Now think of the obvious web design.

10
00:00:37,394 --> 00:00:42,720
A form on ecom's page sends the card number to ecom's server, and ecom passes it on.

11
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:45,816
That one hop puts every card number through ecom.

12
00:00:45,866 --> 00:00:50,040
Then ecom's whole system would need the full check from the last lesson.

13
00:00:50,090 --> 00:00:55,824
So this system does what the real shop does: it puts the bank's card reader on ecom's counter.

14
00:00:55,874 --> 00:00:58,128
Here is how it works, step by step.

15
00:00:58,178 --> 00:01:02,088
First, ecom and the card zone never just talk freely.

16
00:01:02,138 --> 00:01:08,064
They agree on every question they may ask each other, and every answer, written down once.

17
00:01:08,114 --> 00:01:10,176
That agreement is a contract.

18
00:01:10,226 --> 00:01:16,464
ecom can only ask what the contract allows, and the card zone answers only in the shape the contract says.

19
00:01:16,514 --> 00:01:22,464
Second, when a buyer opens the payment page, ecom asks the card zone for a card-entry session.

20
00:01:22,514 --> 00:01:27,456
A card-entry session is a short-lived place to type one card, for one payment.

21
00:01:27,506 --> 00:01:29,736
The card zone answers with an address.

22
00:01:29,786 --> 00:01:33,480
Third, the payment page shows that address inside a frame.

23
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:39,120
The frame is a small window inside the page, and it belongs to the card zone, not to ecom.

24
00:01:39,170 --> 00:01:42,936
That frame is the bank's card reader, standing on ecom's counter.

25
00:01:42,986 --> 00:01:45,912
Fourth, the buyer types the card into the frame.

26
00:01:45,962 --> 00:01:49,296
The card goes straight from the buyer's browser to the card zone.

27
00:01:49,346 --> 00:01:51,240
ecom's servers are not on the way.

28
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:58,920
Fifth, the card zone locks the number away and hands the page a receipt: the card token, the masked number, and the buyer's bank.

29
00:01:58,970 --> 00:02:00,792
Never the number itself.

30
00:02:00,842 --> 00:02:04,824
Sixth, ecom asks the card zone to charge the card by its token.

31
00:02:04,874 --> 00:02:09,960
Before it does, ecom checks that this token really came from this payment's own session.

32
00:02:10,010 --> 00:02:11,832
Now watch the product.

33
00:02:11,882 --> 00:02:15,000
A buyer is sent from the shop to the bank's payment page.

34
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:17,880
At the top it says "Secure payment page of the bank".

35
00:02:17,930 --> 00:02:20,184
It shows the order and the amount to pay.

36
00:02:20,234 --> 00:02:25,104
Below sits a box called "Card details": that is the frame, the bank's reader.

37
00:02:25,154 --> 00:02:27,504
The buyer types the card number and the date.

38
00:02:27,554 --> 00:02:32,088
The box hands back one short line: the masked number and the buyer's bank.

39
00:02:32,138 --> 00:02:35,808
The buyer types the code sent to their phone and confirms.

40
00:02:35,858 --> 00:02:40,080
The page says "Taking the payment", then "Your bank is being asked".

41
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:43,968
A few seconds later it says "Paid", with a reference number.

42
00:02:44,018 --> 00:02:45,840
Now try it the wrong way.

43
00:02:45,890 --> 00:02:50,328
Leave the page open too long, or get the card wrong too many times.

44
00:02:50,378 --> 00:02:56,160
The frame says the attempt ended, too many tries or the time ran out, and asks for the card again.

45
00:02:56,210 --> 00:02:58,584
The session was short-lived on purpose.

46
00:02:58,634 --> 00:03:02,208
Each session binds one card only, and then it is used up.

47
00:03:02,258 --> 00:03:06,384
In the bank admin portal, this payment's row shows a masked number.

48
00:03:06,434 --> 00:03:12,288
That is all ecom ever holds: the token, the mask, the buyer's bank and the card network.

49
00:03:12,338 --> 00:03:14,160
What does this protect?

50
00:03:14,210 --> 00:03:21,120
The buyer's card never passes through ecom, so ecom's servers, logs and backups never hold a card number.

51
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:22,992
A leak there leaks no card.

52
00:03:23,042 --> 00:03:25,056
The shop never touches the card either.

53
00:03:25,106 --> 00:03:29,904
And the bank keeps its full check on one small room, not on ecom's whole system.

54
00:03:29,954 --> 00:03:31,776
But the token still matters.

55
00:03:31,826 --> 00:03:36,048
It can start a payment, so ecom guards it like a ticket that can fetch a coat.

56
00:03:36,098 --> 00:03:38,784
That is why ecom keeps its smaller check.

57
00:03:38,834 --> 00:03:40,656
Back to the counter.

58
00:03:40,706 --> 00:03:43,032
The bank's reader faces the buyer.

59
00:03:43,082 --> 00:03:46,032
The shop keeps receipts in its drawer, never cards.

60
00:03:46,082 --> 00:03:49,056
Next: how every service gets its settings.
